# MobX security

Narrows the common OWASP-style rules to MobX state management. General
web-security concerns come from the common and React rules this file
`extends`; this file covers sensitive data in observable state.

## Do not put secrets in long-lived observable state

- Keep a token, API key, or credential out of an `@observable` field on a store that survives the session (not cleared on logout, or persisted by `autorun`/`reaction` to `localStorage`); pass secrets through a request layer.
- When a `reaction`/`autorun` persists store state to `localStorage`/`sessionStorage`, exclude any field carrying a token, PII, or payment data; persist a derived, scrubbed snapshot, not the whole store.

## Untrusted data into observable state is still untrusted

- Assigning server or user-supplied HTML/markdown into an observable does not sanitize it; sanitize before it reaches the observable if it is later rendered with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (see `rules/security.mdc` in the `react` pack).

## Reaction-driven side effects and SSRF/open-redirect risk

- Validate an observable URL or hostname that a `reaction`/`autorun` uses for `fetch`/navigation as a request-time input would be (e.g. a query param synced into the store).

## Disposal prevents stale-store data leaks across users/sessions

- Call `dispose()` on session/user change for any store holding user-scoped data, to stop rendering or syncing stale, now-unauthorized data (e.g. a lingering `reaction` on a background tab).
